6 . FUNCTIONS :
1. FUNCTIONS ARE SET OF CODE WHICH PERFORMS SOMETHING FOR YOU .
2. FUNCTIONS ARE USED TO MODULARISE CODE .
3. FUNCTIONS ARE USED TO INCREASE READABILITY .
4.FUNCTIONS ARE USED TO USE SAME CODE MULTIPLE TIMES .

A function (or method) in Java is a block of code that performs a specific task.

It helps in code reusability.

Avoids repetition.

Makes the program more modular and readable.

Syntax of a Function :

returnType functionName(parameters) {
    // body of function
    return value; // if returnType is not void
}

int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

🔹 Types of Functions in Java

1. Based on Return Type and Parameters :

There are 4 basic types: 

No return type, No parameters

void greet() {
    System.out.println("Hello!");
}

Only performs an action, doesn’t return a value.


2. No return type, With parameters

void printName(String name) {
    System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
}

Return type, No parameters

int getNumber() {
    return 42;
}


Return type, With parameters

int multiply(int a, int b) {
    return a * b;
}

2. Based on Declaration

User-defined Functions → Written by the programmer.

int square(int x) {
    return x * x;
}

Predefined (Library) Functions → Already available in Java libraries.

Math.sqrt(16);   // returns 4.0
System.out.println("Hello"); // predefined print function

3. Based on Access

Functions can have access modifiers:

public → Accessible everywhere

private → Accessible only within the same class

protected → Accessible within package & subclasses

default → Accessible only within the package

4. Special Types of Functions

Static Functions → Belong to the class, not object.

static void show() {
    System.out.println("Static method");
}


Instance Functions → Need an object to call.

class Example {
    void display() {
        System.out.println("Instance method");
    }
}


Abstract Functions → Declared without body (must be implemented by child class).

abstract class Shape {
    abstract void draw();  // abstract function
}


Final Functions → Cannot be overridden in subclasses.

final void cannotChange() {
    System.out.println("This cannot be overridden");
}


