JAVA PASS BY VALUE AND REFERENCE :

1. Pass by Value

A copy of the actual value is passed to the function.

Changes made inside the function do not affect the original variable.

✅ Example:

class Demo {
    void changeValue(int x) {
        x = 100;  // only local copy is changed
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int a = 50;
        Demo d = new Demo();
        d.changeValue(a);
        System.out.println(a);  // Output: 50 (not changed)
    }
}


👉 Here, a remains 50 because Java passes a copy of a.

2. Pass by Reference (Conceptual Only)

Normally means passing the address (reference) of a variable so the function can modify the original value.

In C++, you can do this with references (int &x).

❌ Java does not support true "pass by reference".
Everything is pass by value in Java.

🔹 Then why confusion in Java?

Because in Java:

Primitive types (int, float, char, etc.) → Copy of value is passed.

Objects → Copy of the reference (memory address) is passed.
That’s why it looks like pass by reference, but technically it’s still pass by value of the reference.

✅ Example with Objects
class Student {
    int marks;
}

class Demo {
    void updateMarks(Student s) {
        s.marks = 100; // modifies the object at that reference
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student st = new Student();
        st.marks = 50;

        Demo d = new Demo();
        d.updateMarks(st);

        System.out.println(st.marks); // Output: 100 (changed!)
    }
}


👉 Here, the reference (address) to st was copied and passed. Both st in main and s in updateMarks point to the same object, so changes reflect.

❌ Misleading Case (Object Reassignment)
class Student {
    int marks;
}

class Demo {
    void reassign(Student s) {
        s = new Student(); // now points to a new object
        s.marks = 200;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student st = new Student();
        st.marks = 50;

        Demo d = new Demo();
        d.reassign(st);

        System.out.println(st.marks); // Output: 50 (not changed)
    }
}


👉 Why? Because the copy of the reference was changed inside the function. The original still points to the old object.

🔹 Final Summary  

Java is strictly Pass by Value.

For primitives → copy of value is passed.

For objects → copy of reference is passed (so object data can change, but reassignment won’t affect original) .

